Q: error using fwrite() to write data structure in c A: <p>Since it is the holiday season, I thought I would review legacy issues to determine if I could provide some enlightenment and an answer if anyone happens to come across these legacy issues.  The originator of this issue may have resolved this issue on their own; however, this seemed to be a good issue to analyze and provide a possible refactored solution.</p>
<p>First, the code was refactored a bit to denote that using &quot;fwrite&quot; to write out structure data should be done in a binary fashion as is usual and customary, and the size of the structure is denoted as this will be the size of the output file when the one structure record is written.</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct
{
    char * name;
    char * regnum;
    int age;
}
student;

int main()
{
    char *studentName = get_string(&quot;What's your name: &quot;);
    char *studentReg = get_string(&quot;Input your Registeration Number: &quot;);
    int age = get_int(&quot;How old are you: &quot;);

    student newStudent = {studentName, studentReg, age};

    FILE *studentinfo = fopen(&quot;Student's_Information.txt&quot;, &quot;wb&quot;);       /* Write as a binary file   */

    if (studentinfo == NULL)
    {
        printf(&quot;Could not open file&quot;);
    }

    printf(&quot;Size of student structure is: %lu\n&quot;, sizeof(student));     /* Denote size of structure */

    fwrite(&amp;newStudent, sizeof(student), 1, studentinfo);

    fclose(studentinfo);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>With that, the program was compiled and executed, storing the data for a student.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/StudentStructure/bin/Release$ ./StudentStructure 
What's your name: Lily
Input your Registeration Number: B-2345-222
How old are you: 20
Size of student structure is: 24
</code></pre>
<p>In checking the properties of the resulting file, it is indeed 24 bytes in size, which should alert one to the fact that the entered text data is not being stored in the file.</p>
<p>To assist with analyzing the file data, a helper program was built to evaluate the data stored within this file.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

#define SIZE 100

typedef struct
{
    char name[100];
    char regnum[100];
    int age;
}
student;

int main(int argc, char * argv[])
{
    student newStudent;

    FILE *studentinfo = fopen(&quot;Student's_Information.txt&quot;, &quot;rb&quot;);

    if (studentinfo == NULL)
    {
        printf(&quot;Could not open file&quot;);
    }

    fread(&amp;newStudent, sizeof(student), 1, studentinfo);

    printf(&quot;%c\n&quot;, argv[1][0]);

    if (argv[1][0] == 'a')
    {
        printf(&quot;Student name...............: %p\n&quot;, newStudent.name);
        printf(&quot;Student registration.......: %p\n&quot;, newStudent.regnum);
        printf(&quot;Student age................: %d\n&quot;, newStudent.age);
    }
    else
    {
        printf(&quot;Student name...............: %s\n&quot;, newStudent.name);
        printf(&quot;Student registration.......: %s\n&quot;, newStudent.regnum);
        printf(&quot;Student age................: %d\n&quot;, newStudent.age);
    }

    fclose(studentinfo);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The program will allow for either printing the binary information stored in this file or text data.  Executing this program to check the binary data, it outputs the original memory location of the name and registration number character arrays along with the age value.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/ReadStudent/bin/Release$ ./ReadStudent a
a
Student name...............: 0x559174bc96b0
Student registration.......: 0x559174bc96f0
Student age................: 20
</code></pre>
<p>If an attempt is made to execute this program and print out text data, the memory address values will be pointing to areas in memory that may produce unexpected characters, or just produce a segment fault.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/ReadStudent/bin/Release$ ./ReadStudent s
s
Segmentation fault (core dumped)
</code></pre>
<p>When researching the use of &quot;fwrite&quot; with the storage of structure data, it is noted that in lieu of character array pointers, character arrays with specific sizes are defined.  With that following is a refactored version of the code with defined character array lengths.</p>
<pre><code>#define SIZE 100

typedef struct
{
    char name[SIZE];            /* Denote a size for the character arrays   */
    char regnum[SIZE];
    int age;
}
student;

int main()
{
    char *studentName = get_string(&quot;What's your name: &quot;);
    char *studentReg = get_string(&quot;Input your Registeration Number: &quot;);
    int age = get_int(&quot;How old are you: &quot;);

    student newStudent;
    strcpy(newStudent.name, studentName);
    strcpy(newStudent.regnum, studentReg);
    newStudent.age = age;

    FILE *studentinfo = fopen(&quot;Student's_Information.txt&quot;, &quot;wb&quot;);       /* Write as a binary file   */

    if (studentinfo == NULL)
    {
        printf(&quot;Could not open file&quot;);
    }

    printf(&quot;Size of student structure is: %lu\n&quot;, sizeof(student));     /* Denote size of structure */

    fwrite(&amp;newStudent, sizeof(student), 1, studentinfo);

    fclose(studentinfo);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>After this refactored code was compiled, it was executed to again prompt for student data and writing to a data file.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/StudentStructure/bin/Release$ ./StudentStructure 
What's your name: Lily
Input your Registeration Number: B-2345-222
How old are you: 20
Size of student structure is: 204
</code></pre>
<p>The thing to note here is the size of the structure being written which alludes to the fact that text data is also being written to the file.</p>
<p>Rerunning the helper program that reads the latest version of the student data file yields the expected result.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/ReadStudent/bin/Release$ ./ReadStudent s
s
Student name...............: Lily
Student registration.......: B-2345-222
Student age................: 20
</code></pre>
<p>The takeaway from this issue is probably more tutorial studying should take place in regard to the storage of structure data in an external file as opposed to just utilizing a structure within a program.</p>
